1. Convert pounds to kilograms:
   \[
   32 \text{ lbs} \times \frac{1 \text{ kg}}{2.2 \text{ lbs}} \approx 14.4 \text{ kg}
   \]

2. Calculate the total loading dose in grams:
   \[
   14.4 \text{ kg} \times 20 \text{ mg/kg} = 288 \text{ mg} = 0.288 \text{ kg}
   \]

3. Convert the flow rate from mg/kg/min to grams/min:
   \[
   0.5 \text{ mg/kg/min} \times frac{1 \text{ kg}}{1000 \text{ mg}} = 0.0005 \text{ kg/min}
   \]

4. Calculate the time required to deliver the load dose:
   \[
   \text{Time} = \dfrac{\text{Total load dose}}{\text{Flow rate}} = \dfrac{0.288 \text{ kg}}{0.0005 \text{ kg/min}} = 576 \text{ minutes}
   \]

