What is the number of chloride ions produced when the complex `[Pt(NH3)4Cl2]Cl2` (tetraamminedichloroplatinum(IV) chloride) is dissolved in an aqueous solution?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The compound `[PT(NH3)4Cl2]Cl2` is tetraamdiochloriphosphoxation(VI) chloride. When this compound is dissolved in an ultraviolet solution, it decomposes into the following components:

- `PT(NH3)4Cl2` (tetraamdiochoxoplasm(VI))
- `Cl2` (two chlorine cells)

The number of chloride ions produced is the sum of the chloride ions from the `PT(Na3)4Cl2` and the `Cl2`.

- `Pt(Na3)4Cl2` has two chloride ions, so it produces two chloride ions.
- `Cl2` is a pair of chloride ions, so it produces two chlorine ions.

Adding these together, the total number of chloride ions produced is:

\[
2 + 2 = 4
\]

Thus, the number of chloride ions produced when the compound is dissolved in an ultraviolet solution is \(\boxed{4}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
