What is the relationship used to estimate the flammability limits of mixtures from the data for individual fuels?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The relationship used to estimate the flammation limits of mixtures from the data for individual factors is the **Law of Limits**, also known as the **Law of Composition**.  

This law states that the flammability limits of a mixture are a linear combination of the flammade limits of the individual components, weighted by their concentrations.  

Mathematically, it is expressed as:

For the **Upper Limit (Upper Limit of the Fading)**:

Upper Limit of Mixture = (Sum of (Upper Limit of Component * Concentration of Component)) / Sum of Concentrations

Or more notationically:

Upper Limit of Mixture = Σ (Upper Limit of Component_i * Concentration_i) / Σ Concentration_i

For the **Lower Limit (Lower Limit of the Falling)**:

Lower Limit of Mixture = (Sum of (Lower Limit of Component * Concentration of Component)) / Sum of Concentrations

Or more notationically:

Lower Limit of Mixture = Σ (Lower Limit of Component_i * Concentration_i) / Σ Concentration_i

Where:

- Lower Limit and Upper Limit are expressed as **percentages of the total mass or total mass weight of the mixture.**
- Component_i is the i-th component in the mixture.
- Concentration_i is the concentration of the i-th component (in the same units for all components).

This formula assumes the components are chemically compatible and do not create any new effects in the mixture.  

\boxed{\text{Lower Limit of Mixture = Σ (Lower Limit of Component_i * Concentration_i) / Σ Concentration_i}}",fluency,"# Task Description
