So, the width of the courtyard is 33/2 m.

The paving stones are 2 1/2 m by some width. We can write 2 1/2 as a fraction:
2 1/2 = 2 + 1/2 = 5/2

So, the paving stones are 5/2 m by some width.

The total area of the courtyard is:
Area of courtyard = length × width
Area of courtyard = 50 m × (33/2) m
Area of courtyard = 1650/2 m²
Area of courtyard = 825 m²

The area of one paving stone is:
Area of paving stone = (5/2) m × width of paving stone

Since there are 165 paving stones, the total area of all paving stones is:
Total area of paving stones = 165 × (5/2) m × width of paving stone

Since the paving stones cover the entire courtyard, the total area of the paving stones must be equal to the area of the courtyard:
165 × (5/2) m × width of paving stone = 825 m²

Now we can solve for the width of each paving stone:
Width of paving stone = 825 m² / (165 × (5/2) m)
Width of paving stone = 825 m² / (825/2 m)
Width of paving stone = 825 m² × (2/825 m)
Width of paving stone = 1650/825 m
Width of paving stone = 2 m

